test_sockleak — pid-exit socket reclaim
=======================================

Opens 7 UDP sockets in a tight loop (just under the per-tid
cap of 8 added in Phase 2), prints a marker, then exits WITHOUT
calling close() on any of them. Running this binary several
times back-to-back exercises PM's inet pid-exit cleanup:

- Without cleanup, slots from each run leak. After enough runs
  the pool fills (32 UDP slots ÷ 7 per run ≈ 4 leaked runs) and
  the next launch's first socket() returns -1.

- With cleanup, PM fans out a PID_EXIT IPC to inet when the
  process ends; inet scans both UDP and TCP tables and frees
  any slot whose `owner_tid` matches.  Each run's 7 slots are
  reclaimed and the next run starts fresh.

The test launches it twice consecutively; both must report
`sockleak: all_opened`.  Cap-enforcement itself is covered by
test_pidcap.

    module oskit.bin.test_sockleak

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall6}


    val NR_SOCKET     = 337

    val AF_INET_V     = 2
    val SOCK_DGRAM_V  = 2


Decimal writer for small ints.

    print_udec(v: int)
        if v >= 10
            print_udec(v / 10)
        var buf: [1]byte
        buf[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&buf[0], 1))


    posix_socket(family: int, stype: int, proto: int) -> int
        int(syscall6(NR_SOCKET, i64(family), i64(stype), i64(proto), 0, 0, 0))


    main(argv: []string)
        val N = 7
        var i = 0
        while i < N
            val fd = posix_socket(AF_INET_V, SOCK_DGRAM_V, 0)
            if fd < 0
                puts("sockleak: failed_at_")
                print_udec(i)
                puts("\n")
                exit_with(1)
                return
            i += 1
        puts("sockleak: all_opened\n")
        // Intentionally exit WITHOUT closing — this is the whole
        // point. If PM's inet pid-exit cleanup works, the slots
        // get reclaimed when the process dies.
        exit()
